{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2985","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2985","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2985\",\"parent_revision\":\"5e92a48d4b4f4ec761cefb67348c12fe73df5bcd551e63fa8ef0e2af3dadde91\",\"root_revision\":\"5e92a48d4b4f4ec761cefb67348c12fe73df5bcd551e63fa8ef0e2af3dadde91\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-18\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA BacklogItem stores its description in two independent locations: the top-level `description` metadata field, and a \\\"Description\\\" entry inside `item.sections` (part of the Story/Description/Acceptance Criteria/Context template every item gets at creation). Both are populated identically at creation time via `add_item`, so they start in sync.\\n\\nCalling `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_update(selector=..., description=\\\"...\\\")` only updates the top-level field. Traced directly in `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py`: `_update_item_description()` (lines 737-748) calls `update_item_metadata(reference, {\\\"description\\\": description})` and returns \u2014 it never touches `item.sections[\\\"Description\\\"]`. The tool's response reports `\\\"description_updated\\\": true` with no indication that a second, independent copy of the same conceptual content was left unchanged.\\n\\nReproduced directly: called `backlog_update(selector=\\\"#2979\\\", description=\\\"<corrected text>\\\")`, got back `{\\\"description_updated\\\": true, \\\"changes\\\": {\\\"description\\\": true}}`. Re-read the item immediately after with `backlog_view` \u2014 the top-level `description` field showed the corrected text, but `sections.Description.entries[0].content` (and the rendered `body`'s `## Description` block \u2014 the part that becomes the actual GitHub issue body) still showed the original, uncorrected text verbatim. Only caught this by manually re-reading the item after the update; the tool's own success response gave no signal that anything was left stale.\\n\\nImpacted functionality: any caller \u2014 human or agent \u2014 using `backlog_update(description=...)` to correct or edit an item's description will silently fail to change what the item's own body/GitHub issue actually displays, while receiving a success response that implies the opposite. `backlog_view`'s compact/summary responses and any consumer reading the top-level `description` field will show corrected content; consumers reading `sections`/`body`/the rendered GitHub issue (the far more common path \u2014 grooming workflows, GitHub's own UI, `backlog view --selector` in close/start.md, etc.) will not.\\n\\nExpected outcome: `backlog_update(description=...)` should update whichever storage location(s) other tools and the rendered body actually read, or (if the two fields are genuinely intended to diverge for some reason not evident from the code read here) the tool's response should explicitly report that the Description section was NOT updated, so a caller isn't misled by a success response into believing the visible content changed.\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog_update's description parameter silently diverges from the rendered description section** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Discovered while correcting backlog items #2979/#2984, 2026-08-18\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-18\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"b4fd2173d88220b19b0125ada243135986a8b1b8ea7c33f36b9128b09ff2253a\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPXnDNg\"}"}